    AUD: { next: "hold", when: "December 8 at 05:30, still the base case, but two hawkish inputs in two days have made it closer than the market has it", note: "The cash rate is 4.85% on TradingEconomics after the November 3 increase from 4.60%, carried 6-3. Today's Q3 wage price index was the second hawkish surprise in two days: plus 1.1% on the quarter against a plus 1.0% forecast, the strongest quarterly rise since 2023, with the prior revised down to 0.7% and the annual rate at 3.5% against 3.4% expected and 3.2% before. Private sector wages rose 1.0% and public sector 1.3%, while real wages fell for a fourth quarter, down 0.4% on the year. That follows November minutes in which six of nine members judged the rate not clearly sufficient to return inflation to target, citing rising inflation pressures, strong services inflation and a likely higher neutral rate, against three dissenters who argued only that it was prudent to wait and assess the 100 basis points already delivered. Against it: TradingEconomics has the market implying an 82% probability rates have peaked and CommBank expects a December hold. October employment lands Thursday 02:30, forecast plus 25,500 after minus 39,329." },
    NZD: { next: "hike", when: "December 9 at 03:00, a full Monetary Policy Statement, and the gap between the path and the currency is now extreme", note: "The official cash rate is 2.75% after October's hold, taken 4-3 against market pricing that had been near 90% for a hike. The committee said tighter policy is likely to be required, depending on the outlook, and flagged near-term inflation uncertainty from volatile oil and fuel prices. Tuesday's Q4 inflation expectations were close to neutral on the gauge that matters: the two-year measure edged to 2.36% from 2.34%, while the one-year jumped to 2.92% from 2.60% and survey respondents put the cash rate at 3.05% by the end of December, which implies a hike. TradingEconomics forecasts 3.00% for December 9 and Westpac looks for December and February moves to a 3.5% neutral, while ANZ has pencilled in a pause. Headline CPI is 3.7% and falling, under the bank's own 3.9% forecast, but its published track has inflation worsening to about 4.4% in Q4. Meanwhile the currency has made a six-year low. Governor Breman speaks at 20:00." },
    CAD: { next: "hold", when: "December 9 at 16:45, a regular decision with no Monetary Policy Report, and the rate market has now moved to the same view", note: "The overnight rate is 2.50% after October's quarter-point increase, the first rise in three years. Implied odds of a December hike have been cut to 38% from 54% before Monday's inflation report, with CIBC and KPMG both expecting a hold and Oxford Economics the main dissent still looking for one more quarter-point move. The bank hiked specifically to stop high energy prices spreading into other goods and services, and energy has fallen about 14% in a month - WTI is 84.73 and down 14.01%, Brent down 15.55%. October CPI did not deliver the acceleration the hike case needed: m/m plus 0.2% against plus 0.3% forecast, trimmed 1.8% against 1.9%, median 2.0% in line, headline y/y held at 3.1% where a Reuters poll looked for 3.2%, and core down to 2.0% from 2.2%, so the two preferred cores now average 1.9%. Set against a six-month-high 6.7% jobless rate and a third consecutive monthly employment fall, a hold is the clear base case. Gravelle speaks at 19:30 and retail sales land Friday 15:30, forecast plus 0.7%." },
    CHF: { next: "hold", when: "December 10 at 10:30, the quarterly assessment, with a hike not expected", note: "The SNB policy rate is 0.00%, unchanged since mid-2025 and held again in September, and previews into December are near-unanimous for another hold. It forecasts average inflation of 0.7% in 2026 and 0.8% in each of 2027 and 2028. October CPI is 1.5%, close to a three-year high, but core is only 0.5%, so the rise is energy. TradingEconomics carries Q3 GDP growth at 0.2% on the quarter, a sharp slowdown from 1.5%, which argues against any tightening impulse. The franc held a modest haven bid for a third session, firmer against the euro, the pound, the Aussie, the Canadian dollar and the kiwi while losing to the dollar and the yen. The official language has softened rather than hardened - the bank removed its prior reference to increased willingness to stem gains in September, noting little safe-haven demand since June, and officials told lawmakers only that they remain prepared to step in - so December is about the intervention wording rather than the rate. Rising Swiss inflation may also make the bank more tolerant of a firmer franc." }
