    { sym: "XAU/USD", bias: "SHORT", conv: "HIGH", why: "3,789.01 and minus 1.57%, a fourteen-month low, down 8.50% on the month. Upgraded from medium because the thesis was tested and paid: a strong Nvidia print did not produce a haven unwind into gold, and the Fed axis got more hawkish on Musalem and Logan rather than less.", risk: "Tonight's minutes at 21:00 are the rates test and a narrow hawkish bloc would bounce this hard off 3,760, which FXEmpire flags as the key support. A Hormuz escalation large enough to re-bid the whole complex is the gap risk, and the news there did worsen overnight." },
    { sym: "XAG/USD", bias: "SHORT", conv: "HIGH", why: "46.09 and minus 2.64%, a thirteen-month low, down 19.26% on the month. This is the clearest confirmation on the board: yesterday this file named a strong Nvidia print as the single risk that would end the forced selling, the print was strong, and silver fell harder than anything here.", risk: "Upgraded from medium on that failed invalidation. The industrial leg can still turn without the Fed if the chip-export curbs are walked back, and a short this extended bounces violently off any dovish line in tonight's minutes. The gold-silver ratio above 82 says the industrial leg, not the Fed, is doing the damage." },
    { sym: "XCU/USD", bias: "SHORT", conv: "MED", why: "5.84 and minus 2.27%, down 10.67% on the month and still plus 15.91% on the year. Upgraded from low-med for the same reason as silver: the Nvidia beat was supposed to relieve the industrial-metals selling and copper fell anyway, which is also why the Aussie could not use its wage beat.", risk: "Still paper against a tight physical market, which caps conviction below the metals shorts: Shanghai warehouse stocks have been near their lowest since late December and the Yangshan import premium hit a record 285 dollars a tonne. Chinese stimulus or a reversal of the chip-export curbs would bite quickly." },
    { sym: "USOIL", bias: "SHORT", conv: "LOW-MED", why: "84.73 and minus 1.49%, down 14.01% on the month, reversing yesterday's bounce. The notable thing is what it fell through: Iran said three tankers struck mines on Tuesday, a daily tracker calls the strait effectively closed and Kpler puts flows at about 48% of pre-war - and the price still fell.", risk: "That is the strongest version of the premium-unwind thesis and also the reason conviction stays capped: a market discounting live escalation is a market that can gap when one lands. The IEA still forecasts a fourth-quarter surplus and Goldman sees Brent averaging 88 dollars in 2027, which is the slower support for the short." }
